(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Many patients with anginal symptoms and angiographically-unobstructed coronaries) Tj
T*
(experience persistent illness-related distress. We randomized 250 participants \(mean age) Tj
T*
(63 years, 50% women\) to receive either targeted management guided by cardiac stress) Tj
T*
(imaging \(n=124\) or conventional angiography-based care \(n=126\). Illness perception was) Tj
T*
(assessed using standardized instruments at baseline and at 6 and 12-month intervals. At 6) Tj
T*
(months, participants receiving imaging-directed care reported substantially lower illness) Tj
T*
(perception scores \(adjusted mean difference -8.8 points, 95% CI -12.3 to -5.4, p<0.001\)) Tj
T*
(compared with controls. This improvement was maintained at 12 months \(adjusted mean) Tj
T*
(difference -11.0 points, 95% CI -14.6 to -7.4, p<0.001\). Psychological distress symptoms) Tj
T*
(also showed greater reductions in the imaging-guided group at both 6 months \(adjusted mean) Tj
T*
(difference -0.9 points, p<0.001\) and 12 months \(adjusted mean difference -1.0 points,) Tj
T*
(p<0.001\). The magnitude and persistence of benefit over 12 months highlight the importance) Tj
T*
(of diagnostic clarity in reducing psychological burden.) Tj
